  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...This morning, the cold front tracks eastwards \nthrough the region, although it will slow down as it reaches \nwestern New England. While the best upper forcing will remain \nnorthwest of the region, low-level convergence along the front \nmay be able to help initiate a few showers or thunderstorms. \nLatest HREF suggests 500 to maybe 1000 J/kg of SBCAPE ahead of \nthe cold front overlapping with 40-45 kt of deep-layer shear in\nwestern New England. Hodographs are fairly long with some \ncounter- clockwise curvature thanks to backing winds with \nheight. This would generally be a relatively favorable \nenvironment for severe weather with splitting cells and even \nsome left movers. However, abundant mid-level dry air and \nsubsidence will be very difficult for any updrafts to overcome \nand be able to sustain themselves. 700-500 mb lapse rates are \nalso unimpressive, with a mid-level cap developing this \nafternoon. So, these factors will help mitigate the severe \nweather threat. SPC has clipped far southeastern Berkshire \nCounty and portions of Litchfield County with the marginal risk \nfor severe weather today, but the threat is very conditional on \nupdrafts being able to overcome the mid- level dry air. \n\nWhile a few showers are possible across the ADKs today closer to\nthe upper trough and due to some westerly upslope enhancement,\nmost of the rest of the region should see a drying trend with\nlowering humidity through the day. Highs will mainly be in the\n70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...All areas dry out tonight as sfc high pressure\nbuilds into the region from the west. The sfc high will remain\noverhead or just to our east through Saturday morning. During\nthis period, we will see comfortable humidity and quiet weather.\nDaytime highs Thursday will only be in the 70s with the upper\ntrough still overhead, but warm into the 70s to 80s Friday as\nheights rise aloft. Overnight lows will be quite comfortable\neach night with 40s to 50s. We went cooler than the NBM Thursday\nnight and Friday night with the sfc high overhead. Some patchy\nradiation fog will also be possible in the typical valley areas\neach night as well.\n\nSaturday into early next week, the sfc high slides off to our\neast, setting up S/SW return flow into our region. Temperatures\nrise slightly above normal into the mid 80s for valley areas\nSaturday through Monday. Dew points remain fairly comfortable,\nbut do begin to creep up as well especially Sunday into Monday.\nAnother approaching upper trough and diffluent flow aloft may\nlead to renewed shower chances late Sunday and early next week,\nbut none of these days currently look to be a washout.",
